From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 17:03:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] libhealthloop: the power supply uevent filter needs a 5.3
 verifier

skfilter_power_supply scans for "\0SUBSYSTEM=" with a ~220 iteration
loop. The comment says BPF loops are unrolled, but at roughly 25
instructions an iteration a full unroll does not fit in BPF_MAXINSNS
(4096), so the compiler emits a real loop. Verifying a bounded loop
needs kernel 5.3; on 4.9 the verifier rejects the program:

  BPF_PROG_LOAD call for /vendor/etc/bpf/filterPowerSupplyEvents.o
      (skfilter_power_supply) returned fd: -1 (Invalid argument)
  BPF_PROG_LOAD - BEGIN log_buf contents:
  back-edge from insn 7 to 8

This .o is CRITICAL("healthd"), and as the comment on
DEFINE_BPF_PROG_KVER_RANGE_OPT spells out, a non-optional program that
fails to load in a critical .o terminates the bpfloader without setting
bpf.progs_loaded, "which in turn results in waitForProgsLoaded() never
finishing". Its own HealthLoop is one of the callers that then blocks:
UeventInit() waits there forever, so the health HAL never reaches
MainLoop() to poll binder, BatteryService.onStart() hangs in
IHealth.registerCallback() on system_server's main thread, and the
watchdog kills system_server every boot.

Declare the kernel version this program actually requires so an older
one skips it, which is not a failure. UeventInit() already handles the
filter being absent -- it logs a warning and receives unfiltered uevents.

---
 health/utils/libhealthloop/filterPowerSupplyEvents.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/health/utils/libhealthloop/filterPowerSupplyEvents.c b/health/utils/libhealthloop/filterPowerSupplyEvents.c
index 6f295c7098..aa90713cd6 100644
--- a/health/utils/libhealthloop/filterPowerSupplyEvents.c
+++ b/health/utils/libhealthloop/filterPowerSupplyEvents.c
@@ -32,7 +32,17 @@ static long (*bpf_skb_load_bytes)(const struct __sk_buff* skb, int off, void* to
 #define HEX_POWER_SU 0x75735f7265776f70uLL  // "power_su"
 #define HEX_PPLY0    0x00000000796c7070uLL  // "pply\0" + null padding
 
-DEFINE_BPF_PROG("skfilter/power_supply", AID_ROOT, AID_SYSTEM, skfilter_power_supply)
+// lineage-24.0: the loop below is a back-edge unless the compiler unrolls it, and at ~220
+// iterations it cannot unroll within BPF_MAXINSNS (4096). Verifying a bounded loop needs kernel
+// 5.3; a 4.9 verifier rejects the program outright with "back-edge from insn 7 to 8". This .o is
+// CRITICAL, and per the comment on DEFINE_BPF_PROG_KVER_RANGE_OPT a non-optional program failing
+// in a critical .o terminates the bpfloader without setting bpf.progs_loaded, so every
+// waitForProgsLoaded() caller blocks forever -- HealthLoop itself among them, which then never
+// polls binder and hangs BatteryService in IHealth.registerCallback(). Declare the real floor so
+// an older kernel skips the program instead: HealthLoop::UeventInit() already treats a missing
+// filter as a warning and just receives unfiltered uevents.
+DEFINE_BPF_PROG_KVER("skfilter/power_supply", AID_ROOT, AID_SYSTEM, skfilter_power_supply,
+                     KVER(5, 3, 0))
 (struct __sk_buff* skb) {
     // The first character matched is a '\0'. Starting right past the netlink message header
     // is fine since the SUBSYSTEM= text never occurs at the start, as the kernel always
